---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
---
<Page title="doze Kafka" theme="kafka" current="kafka">
<div class="kf kf-s1 kf-root">

  <div class="kf-sub kf-s2">
    <nav class="kf-s3" aria-label="doze Kafka">
      <span class="kf-s4"><span class="kf-s5"></span>doze Kafka</span>
      <a href="#clients">Clients</a><a href="#features">What you get</a><a href="#not">What it isn't</a><a href="#install">Install</a><a href="/docs/kafka/">Docs</a><a href="/changelog/">Changelog</a>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <section class="kf-s6">
    <div class="kf-s7">
      <img class="kf-s8" src="/brand/doze-kafka.svg" alt="doze Kafka">
      <span class="kf-s9">One Go binary · single node · disk-backed</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="kf-hero-title kf-s10">Kafka for your laptop. Hold the JVM.</h1>
    <p class="kf-s11">A native broker that boots in milliseconds, sits quiet until a client connects, keeps your data on disk, and shuts down cleanly. No JVM, no ZooKeeper, no KRaft quorum, no Docker.</p>
    <div class="kf-s12"><span class="kf-s13">-Xmx1G</span><span class="kf-s13">waiting for controller quorum…</span><span class="kf-s13">all you wanted was one topic</span></div>
    <div class="kf-stack-sm kf-s14">
      <a class="kf-btn kf-btn-sun" href="#install">Install doze Kafka</a>
      <a class="kf-btn kf-btn-ghost" href="/docs/kafka/">Getting started</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Clients -->
  <section class="kf-s15" id="clients">
    <div class="kf-s16">
      <h2 class="kf-s17">If your client can't tell, it's Kafka.</h2>
      <p class="kf-s18">Built to behave like Kafka to any client on the socket. These are tested against it in CI on every change:</p>
      <div class="kf-s19">
        <span class="kf-pill">franz-go</span><span class="kf-pill">Sarama</span><span class="kf-pill">Java client</span><span class="kf-pill">Kafka Streams</span><span class="kf-pill">Spring Kafka</span><span class="kf-pill">librdkafka · kcat · confluent-kafka</span><span class="kf-pill">KafkaJS</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The real console -->
  <section class="kf-s20">
    <div class="kf-s21">
      <span class="kf-s9">The console</span>
      <h2 class="kf-s22">See every record. And why it's there.</h2>
      <p class="kf-s23">Topics, the tape of records on disk, messages decoded, and underneath, the requests that touched them. On by default.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="kf-s24">
      <div class="kf-s25"></div>
      <div class="kf-s26">
        <div class="kf-s27"><span class="kf-s28"></span><span class="kf-s29"></span><span class="kf-s30"></span><span class="kf-s31">doze Kafka · console</span></div>
        <img class="kf-s32" src="/media/kafka-orders.webp" alt="The doze Kafka console: a topic, its tape of 40 records on disk, the messages, and the requests underneath">
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="kf-s33">The real console, from its demo stack: 40 records produced with kcat. <span class="kf-recap">[re-capture with Larder]</span></p>
    <div class="kf-s34">
      <div class="kf-s35">
        <h3 class="kf-s36">Live, as it lands.</h3>
        <p class="kf-s37">kcat produces records; each request shows up in the traffic, then on the topic's tape, newest on the right.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="kf-s38">
        <video class="kf-s39" src="/media/kafka-live.webm" autoplay="autoplay" muted="muted" loop="loop" playsinline="playsinline" controls="controls" aria-label="Records produced with kcat appear in doze Kafka's traffic and on the topic's tape"></video>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Quickstart -->
  <section class="kf-s40">
    <div class="kf-s41">
      <h2 class="kf-s42">It's a name, not a port</h2>
      <p class="kf-s23">Each broker answers on its own loopback address with a name, so nothing on your network can reach it and two projects can both use 9092. Stop it with Ctrl-C: topics, messages and committed offsets are all there next time.</p>
    </div>
    <pre class="kf-s43"><span class="kf-s44">$</span> cd ~/code/larder &amp;&amp; doze-kafka
  broker   <span class="kf-s45">kafka.larder.doze:9092</span>
  console  <span class="kf-s45">http://kafka.larder.doze</span>

<span class="kf-s44">$</span> kcat -b kafka.larder.doze:9092 -t orders.placed -P
<span class="kf-s44">$</span> kcat -b kafka.larder.doze:9092 -t orders.placed -C -e
&#123;"order_id": "[id]", "total": "[amount]"&#125;</pre>
  </section>

  <!-- In Larder -->
  <section class="kf-s46">
    <div class="kf-s47">
      <div class="kf-s48">
        <span class="kf-s9">Inside a doze stack</span>
        <h2 class="kf-s49">The topics are the contracts.</h2>
        <p class="kf-s37">In Larder, the example grocer, services in Go, Python and TypeScript talk through four topics, declared in doze.hcl rather than created by whichever client writes first. franz-go, confluent-kafka and KafkaJS, all on one broker.</p>
        <div class="kf-s12"><span class="kf-topic">users.signed-up</span><span class="kf-topic">orders.placed</span><span class="kf-topic">payments.settled</span><span class="kf-topic">payments.failed</span></div>
        <a class="kf-s50" href="/examples/">See Larder →</a>
      </div>
      <pre class="kf-s51"><span class="kf-s44"># doze.hcl, from Larder</span>
module kafka "kafka" &#123;
  version            = 4
  port               = 9092
  auto_create_topics = false

  topic "users.signed-up"  &#123; partitions = 3 &#125;
  topic "orders.placed"    &#123; partitions = 3 &#125;
  topic "payments.settled" &#123; partitions = 3 &#125;
  topic "payments.failed"  &#123; partitions = 3 &#125;
&#125;</pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Features -->
  <section class="kf-s52" id="features">
    <div class="kf-s53">
      <h2 class="kf-s54">What you get</h2>
      <div class="kf-s55">
        <div class="kf-feat"><h3 class="kf-s56">Wire protocol Kafka 1–4</h3><p>One binary speaks Kafka 1 to 4; pick which with <code class="kf-s57">--kafka-version</code> (default 4).</p></div>
        <div class="kf-feat"><h3 class="kf-s56">Consumer groups</h3><p>The classic protocol and KIP-848, chosen by the client.</p></div>
        <div class="kf-feat"><h3 class="kf-s56">A schema registry, built in</h3><p>Confluent-compatible: Avro, JSON Schema and Protobuf. Stock serializers need only schema.registry.url.</p></div>
        <div class="kf-feat"><h3 class="kf-s56">A console that explains</h3><p>Topics, messages, lag, and why a group keeps rebalancing, with the Kafka error names.</p></div>
        <div class="kf-feat"><h3 class="kf-s56">Transactions, SASL and TLS</h3><p>Transactional producers and read-committed consumers. SASL/PLAIN, SCRAM-256/512 and TLS when you want them.</p></div>
        <div class="kf-feat"><h3 class="kf-s56">Durable</h3><p>With acks=all an acknowledged write is fsynced before the ack.</p></div>
        <div class="kf-feat"><h3 class="kf-s56">Flat memory</h3><p>Idle memory stays in the tens of MB however much data sits on disk.</p></div>
        <div class="kf-feat"><h3 class="kf-s56">Embeddable in Go tests</h3><p><code class="kf-s57">server.New(...)</code> starts a broker on a free port, nothing to clean up.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What it isn't -->
  <section class="kf-s58" id="not">
    <div class="kf-s48">
      <h2 class="kf-s59">What it isn't, on purpose</h2>
      <p class="kf-s37">A single node for development, not production. Anything a client can't observe through the socket is left out.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="kf-s60">
      <li>No replication, ISR or controller quorum</li>
      <li>No byte-for-byte on-disk segment compatibility</li>
      <li>No tiered storage or quotas</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Install -->
  <section class="kf-s61" id="install">
    <div class="kf-stack-sm kf-inst kf-s62">
      <div class="kf-s63">
        <h2 class="kf-s64">Run it inside doze, or alone</h2>
        <span class="kf-s65">In a doze stack</span>
        <div class="kf-cmd"><code>doze add kafka</code><button data-copy="doze add kafka" type="button" class="kf-copy" aria-label="Copy doze add kafka">Copy</button></div>
        <span class="kf-s65">On its own: Linux and macOS, amd64 and arm64</span>
        <span class="kf-s66">The standalone broker runs on Intel Macs too. doze itself, and so <code class="kf-s57">doze add kafka</code>, needs Apple silicon on a Mac.</span>
        <div class="kf-cmd"><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/doze-dev/doze-kafka/main/install.sh | sh</code><button data-copy="curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/doze-dev/doze-kafka/main/install.sh | sh" type="button" class="kf-copy" aria-label="Copy the install command">Copy</button></div>
        <span class="kf-s67">More ways, and doze itself: <a class="kf-s68" href="/download/">Download</a></span>
      </div>
      <img class="kf-s69" src="/brand/doze-kafka.svg" alt="">
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="kf-s70">Apache Kafka is a trademark of the Apache Software Foundation. doze Kafka is not affiliated with it.</p>
  </div>
</Page>

